#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088620} :activity/id #uuid "76ca2b80-d1c4-56ac-a487-16d19a3f6f0c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048900 :topic/id #uuid "ca65b01a-1b26-51b3-b923-b5a1e97a0e03" :topic/math-academy-id 893} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088936 :tutorial/content "Consider the complex number $z=3+4\\textrm{i},$ as depicted on the Argand diagram below:\n\n![](images/c8/c857172451c97c24caab685a2f52097c0ca3c83df212c46a21f4a49c3edb19ca.png)\n\nThere is a natural angle $\\theta$ that $z$ makes with the positive real axis. This angle is called the **argument** of $z.$ We often denote the argument as $\\arg(z),$ or just $\\theta.$ The numeric value of $\\arg(z)$ is given in radians, and we restrict the values to \n\n$$\n0\\leq \\arg(z) \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIn the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.93,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. Therefore $\\arg (z) \\approx 0.93$ to two decimal places.\n\n**Caution:** When you evaluate $\\arctan \\left( \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ on your calculator, be sure that you're in radians mode, and be sure to put parentheses around the $\\dfrac{4}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f2fefd3c-b47b-5b28-88a0-1c4769eb1e11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3731 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "19aceb75-1b53-51bf-af47-c2f1204b2d1c" :step/math-academy-id 20973 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088621}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087757 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087573 :question/id #uuid "a930b12e-d598-584e-82cc-63efb6c91e25" :question/math-academy-id "e-5773"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8cc1de8-5ac0-5607-a2eb-6eec375721e8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17